# harness-config: single source of truth for the DSH harness across all machines.
# See README.md.

# machine-local / secrets -- never commit
*.bak-*
.credentials.yaml
.anonymous-user-id

# python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/

# dsh runtime state, if ever mirrored here
sessions/
profiles/node_modules/
storages/

# os
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
checkmerge.py

# per-machine hidden-task launchers, regenerated by scripts/Set-TaskHidden.ps1
scripts/hidden-tasks/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERATED JOURNAL STATE -- not tracked, on purpose (journal P2609)
#
# journal/index/* is a pure cache: `journal.py` rebuilds it on demand when it is missing or
# stale (`index_dir()`, `stamp.json`), so it has no content value in git. Tracking it made
# every host dirty the moment it merely USED the journal, which blocked fast-forwards -- on
# 2026-09-28 the only two files blocking a 56-commit fast-forward were
# `journal/index/stamp.json` and `journal/state/owner-questions.md`. `journal.db` alone was
# 16 MB of generated SQLite. `journal/index/README.md` is documentation and stays tracked.
#
# 2026-10-21 (P1): the rule above was right and HALF-ENFORCED -- journal.db and entries.tsv
# were still tracked, so the ignore did nothing for them and every append committed a fresh
# ~33.7 MB database. Measured in history: 3,085 blobs / 15.2 GB under journal/index, and the
# ten largest objects in the whole repository were all journal.db. The consequence was not
# storage, it was that `git fetch` on this repo could not finish inside any sane timeout, and
# the cross-machine id ceiling depended on that fetch. Both are now untracked for real, so the
# ignore below applies to them. `aliases.tsv` and `stamp.json` STAY TRACKED: aliases are not
# derivable from entries/, and stamp.json is a few hundred bytes of per-host ceiling hint that
# other code reads. Do not "finish the job" by untracking those without giving aliases a source
# of truth first.
index/aliases.tsv
index/entries.tsv
index/journal.db
index/journal.db-*
index/stamp.json

# journal/state/owner-questions.md is a MIRROR generated by `journal.py questions` from the
# `owner_decision_queue` table on the authority. The TABLE is the source of truth; the mirror
# is a convenience for a host that cannot reach the authority. Untrack the mirror so refreshing
# it does not dirty the tree, and keep every other file in state/ tracked -- those are records
# (status.tsv is the append-only status tier; in-flight.md is rewritten state a session reads).
state/owner-questions.md
